From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: waydroid-nvidia-prolapse <build@local>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:12:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 16/20] hwcomposer: never send a display hotplug from the
 present path

hwc_set() called procs->hotplug(0, 1) whenever an xdg_toplevel configure
(resize, or a state change such as activation) had set needHotplug. Older
HWC2On1Adapters dropped display-0 hotplugs, so this was a no-op. The
lineage-20.0 20260927 vendor image carries Waydroid's "hwc2on1: Handle
primary hotplugs" patch, which forwards it as CONNECTED. IComposerCallback
onHotplug is synchronous, so the callback is a nested binder call that
runs on SurfaceFlinger's main thread, which is inside
HWComposer::presentAndGetReleaseFences. SurfaceFlinger handles a
main-thread hotplug at once (processDisplayHotplugEventsLocked ->
processDisplayChangesLocked -> Display::disconnect ->
HWComposer::disconnectDisplay -> mDisplayData.erase), then dereferences
the erased DisplayData for getReleaseFences(): SEGV at
presentAndGetReleaseFences+807 (core of 2026-10-16 17:15:45: the frame's
DisplayData node is freed and zeroed, mDisplayData holds a new node for
the same display). The composer also purged that display's buffer cache
(ComposerClient cleanDisplayResources) in the middle of its own present.

The display's configs are fixed at calibration (build_display_modes runs
once), so the hotplug carried no information. Drop it and needHotplug;
do_hotplug() keeps its touch-pipe/property work and the invalidate nudge.

Regression guard: #pragma GCC poison hotplug in hwcomposer.cpp and
wayland-hwc.cpp (the two files that hold hwc_procs_t) makes any new
procs->hotplug call a build error.
---
 hwcomposer/hwcomposer.cpp  | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++------
 hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.cpp | 19 ++++++++++++++-----
 hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.h   |  5 ++---
 3 files changed, 36 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hwcomposer/hwcomposer.cpp b/hwcomposer/hwcomposer.cpp
index 53de76f..9adeda1 100644
--- a/hwcomposer/hwcomposer.cpp
+++ b/hwcomposer/hwcomposer.cpp
@@ -62,6 +62,11 @@
 #include "modes/multi-window.h"
 #include "modes/single-window.h"
 
+/* Regression guard (SurfaceFlinger crash 2026-10-16, see hwc_set()): this HAL
+ * must never call hwc_procs_t::hotplug. Any use of the identifier after this
+ * point fails the build. */
+#pragma GCC poison hotplug
+
 using ::android::hardware::configureRpcThreadpool;
 using ::android::hardware::joinRpcThreadpool;
 
@@ -657,12 +662,21 @@ static int hwc_set(struct hwc_composer_device_1* dev,size_t numDisplays,
         refit_windows_to_display(pdev);
     }
 
-    if (pdev->display->needHotplug && pdev->procs && pdev->procs->hotplug) {
-        pdev->procs->hotplug(pdev->procs, 0, 1);
-        /* No buffer_map.clear(): the buffers stay valid across a hotplug, and
-         * prune_stale_cached_buffers() drops the ones that stop being used. */
-        pdev->display->needHotplug = false;
-    }
+    /* No procs->hotplug() here, ever. This function runs inside
+     * SurfaceFlinger's presentDisplay call: the HWC2On1Adapter (with
+     * Waydroid's "hwc2on1: Handle primary hotplugs" patch, in vendor images
+     * since 20260927) forwards a display-0 hotplug as CONNECTED, the HIDL
+     * callback is a nested binder call that lands on SurfaceFlinger's main
+     * thread, which is blocked in HWComposer::presentAndGetReleaseFences,
+     * and SurfaceFlinger processes a main-thread hotplug immediately:
+     * processDisplayChangesLocked() recreates the display and erases the
+     * HWComposer::DisplayData that presentAndGetReleaseFences still holds a
+     * reference to. It then calls getReleaseFences() through the freed entry
+     * (SEGV in presentAndGetReleaseFences, 2026-10-16).
+     * The display's configs never change after calibration
+     * (build_display_modes() runs once), so a hotplug would carry no new
+     * information anyway; resolution changes go through display modes
+     * (match_display_mode_to_window). See do_hotplug(). */
     return 0;
 }
 
diff --git a/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.cpp b/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.cpp
index be44bd9..5d4a5d2 100644
--- a/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.cpp
+++ b/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.cpp
@@ -85,6 +85,11 @@
 #include "fractional-scale-v1-client-protocol.h"
 #include "xdg-decoration-unstable-v1-client-protocol.h"
 
+/* Regression guard (SurfaceFlinger crash 2026-10-16, see hwc_set()): this HAL
+ * must never call hwc_procs_t::hotplug. Any use of the identifier after this
+ * point fails the build. */
+#pragma GCC poison hotplug
+
 using ::android::hardware::hidl_string;
 
 /* See display::last_input_serial: keyboard and pointer events do not reach the
@@ -262,8 +267,10 @@ do_hotplug(struct display *display) {
         mkfifo(INPUT_PIPE_NAME[INPUT_TOUCH], S_IRUSR | S_IWUSR | S_IRGRP | S_IWGRP);
         chown(INPUT_PIPE_NAME[INPUT_TOUCH], 1000, 1000);
     }
+    /* Despite the name, no HWC hotplug is sent: the configs SurfaceFlinger
+     * read at startup stay valid (see hwc_set()). The window geometry changed,
+     * so ask SurfaceFlinger for a frame that re-positions the layers. */
     if (display->procs && display->procs->invalidate) {
-        display->needHotplug = true;
         /* See display::creatingWindow — calling into SurfaceFlinger here while
          * the composer thread is blocked in window::create() deadlocks both. */
         if (!display->creatingWindow.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
@@ -291,10 +298,12 @@ toplevel_has_state(struct wl_array *states, uint32_t wanted)
  *
  * Android hot-resizes its display all the time — it is how foldables switch
  * panels (DisplayFoldController -> WindowManagerInternal::setForcedDisplaySize).
- * What is NOT available to us is the hwc route: do_hotplug()'s
- * procs->hotplug(0, 1) would make SurfaceFlinger re-read the display
- * attributes, but the HWC2On1Adapter discards hotplug for display 0
- * ("Received hotplug for non-external display"). WindowManager's shell command
+ * What is NOT available to us is the hwc route: a display-0 procs->hotplug(0, 1)
+ * is dropped by older HWC2On1Adapters ("Received hotplug for non-external
+ * display") and, with Waydroid's primary-hotplug adapter patch, makes
+ * SurfaceFlinger tear down and recreate the whole display (which crashed it
+ * when sent from inside present; hwc never sends one, see hwc_set()).
+ * WindowManager's shell command
  * is the entry point a vendor HAL can actually reach; setForcedDisplaySize
  * requires WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS, which this process (uid 1000, system) holds.
  *
diff --git a/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.h b/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.h
index 44f9cd3..b13c2b6 100644
--- a/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.h
+++ b/hwcomposer/wayland-hwc.h
@@ -536,14 +536,13 @@ struct display {
     sp<IWaydroidTask> task;
 
     const hwc_procs_t *procs;
-    bool needHotplug;
     /* Set while the composer thread is inside window::create(), which blocks
      * waiting for the compositor's first configure. do_hotplug() must not call
      * procs->invalidate() during that window: invalidate re-enters
      * SurfaceFlinger, which comes back into hwc on the very thread that is
      * blocked, and both threads wedge (composer in wl_display_roundtrip_queue,
-     * Wayland thread in do_hotplug). needHotplug still gets set, and hwc_set
-     * consumes it on the next frame, so nothing is lost by skipping the nudge. */
+     * Wayland thread in do_hotplug). Skipping the nudge loses nothing: the
+     * next frame SurfaceFlinger presents re-positions the layers anyway. */
     std::atomic<bool> creatingWindow;
 };
 
